Relevant columns and their potential usage

• “family_id” (PUBLICATIONS): groups all related publications; filter out ‘-1’, rank by count  
• “publication_number” (PUBLICATIONS & ABS_AND_EMB): join key between the two tables  
• “abstract” (ABS_AND_EMB): text we need; keep rows where it is not NULL / not empty  
• “title” (ABS_AND_EMB): may help inspection, but not required for the final answer  
• “country_code”, “kind_code”, etc. (PUBLICATIONS): optional for extra slicing

Typical conditions that will be useful

1. "family_id" IS NOT NULL AND "family_id" <> '-1'        -- ignore missing / default id  
2. ABS."abstract" IS NOT NULL AND ABS."abstract" <> ''    -- keep non‑empty abstracts  
3. LATER JOIN on PUBLICATIONS."publication_number" = ABS."publication_number"  
4. ORDER BY COUNT(*) DESC                                 -- rank by family size  
5. LIMIT …                                               -- sample or top‑N

Below are ten Snowflake SQL queries, progressing from basic exploration to the complete pull.  
(All column and object names are enclosed in double quotes; each query returns ≤20 rows for illustration.)

```sql
-- 1. Quick look at family_id values (distinct sample)
SELECT DISTINCT "family_id"
FROM PATENTS_GOOGLE.PATENTS_GOOGLE.PUBLICATIONS
WHERE "family_id" IS NOT NULL
  AND "family_id" <> '-1'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Count how many publications belong to each family (unsorted sample)
SELECT "family_id",
       COUNT(*) AS "pub_cnt"
FROM PATENTS_GOOGLE.PATENTS_GOOGLE.PUBLICATIONS
WHERE "family_id" IS NOT NULL
  AND "family_id" <> '-1'
GROUP BY "family_id"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Same as #2 but sorted to see the largest families
SELECT "family_id",
       COUNT(*) AS "pub_cnt"
FROM PATENTS_GOOGLE.PATENTS_GOOGLE.PUBLICATIONS
WHERE "family_id" IS NOT NULL
  AND "family_id" <> '-1'
GROUP BY "family_id"
ORDER BY "pub_cnt" DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Inspect a couple of publication numbers inside a big family
SELECT "family_id",
       "publication_number"
FROM PATENTS_GOOGLE.PATENTS_GOOGLE.PUBLICATIONS
WHERE "family_id" = (  -- substitute with one family_id returned by query #3
        SELECT "family_id"
        FROM PATENTS_GOOGLE.PATENTS_GOOGLE.PUBLICATIONS
        WHERE "family_id" IS NOT NULL
          AND "family_id" <> '-1'
        GROUP BY "family_id"
        ORDER BY COUNT(*) DESC
        LIMIT 1 )
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Preview abstracts in ABS_AND_EMB (stand‑alone)
SELECT "publication_number",
       "abstract"
FROM PATENTS_GOOGLE.PATENTS_GOOGLE.ABS_AND_EMB
WHERE "abstract" IS NOT NULL
  AND "abstract" <> ''
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Join one family to its abstracts (sample)
SELECT PUB."family_id",
       PUB."publication_number",
       ABS."abstract"
FROM PATENTS_GOOGLE.PATENTS_GOOGLE.PUBLICATIONS AS PUB
JOIN PATENTS_GOOGLE.PATENTS_GOOGLE.ABS_AND_EMB AS ABS
  ON PUB."publication_number" = ABS."publication_number"
WHERE PUB."family_id" = (     -- same placeholder as #4
        SELECT "family_id"
        FROM PATENTS_GOOGLE.PATENTS_GOOGLE.PUBLICATIONS
        WHERE "family_id" IS NOT NULL
          AND "family_id" <> '-1'
        GROUP BY "family_id"
        ORDER BY COUNT(*) DESC
        LIMIT 1 )
  AND ABS."abstract" IS NOT NULL
  AND ABS."abstract" <> ''
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Top six families by publication count
WITH ranked AS (
  SELECT "family_id",
         COUNT(*) AS "pub_cnt",
         ROW_NUMBER() OVER (ORDER BY COUNT(*) DESC) AS rnk
  FROM PATENTS_GOOGLE.PATENTS_GOOGLE.PUBLICATIONS
  WHERE "family_id" IS NOT NULL
    AND "family_id" <> '-1'
  GROUP BY "family_id"
)
SELECT "family_id", "pub_cnt"
FROM ranked
WHERE rnk <= 6
ORDER BY "pub_cnt" DESC;
```

```sql
-- 8. Get every publication_number that belongs to those top six families
WITH top6 AS (
  SELECT "family_id"
  FROM (
        SELECT "family_id",
               COUNT(*) AS "pub_cnt"
        FROM PATENTS_GOOGLE.PATENTS_GOOGLE.PUBLICATIONS
        WHERE "family_id" IS NOT NULL
          AND "family_id" <> '-1'
        GROUP BY "family_id"
        ORDER BY "pub_cnt" DESC
        LIMIT 6
       )
)
SELECT PUB."family_id",
       PUB."publication_number"
FROM PATENTS_GOOGLE.PATENTS_GOOGLE.PUBLICATIONS AS PUB
JOIN top6
  ON PUB."family_id" = top6."family_id"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Join top six families with abstract text, require non‑empty abstract
WITH top6 AS (
  SELECT "family_id"
  FROM (
        SELECT "family_id",
               COUNT(*) AS "pub_cnt"
        FROM PATENTS_GOOGLE.PATENTS_GOOGLE.PUBLICATIONS
        WHERE "family_id" IS NOT NULL
          AND "family_id" <> '-1'
        GROUP BY "family_id"
        ORDER BY "pub_cnt" DESC
        LIMIT 6
       )
)
SELECT PUB."family_id",
       PUB."publication_number",
       ABS."abstract"
FROM PATENTS_GOOGLE.PATENTS_GOOGLE.PUBLICATIONS AS PUB
JOIN top6
  ON PUB."family_id" = top6."family_id"
JOIN PATENTS_GOOGLE.PATENTS_GOOGLE.ABS_AND_EMB AS ABS
  ON PUB."publication_number" = ABS."publication_number"
WHERE ABS."abstract" IS NOT NULL
  AND ABS."abstract" <> ''
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. Final pull: each of the top six family_ids with every non‑empty abstract (no LIMIT here if you’re ready)
WITH top6 AS (
